WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.230
All strategic leaders face complexity
in their competitive environments.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.050
What you see is enormous variety of

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.390
potentially disruptive processes
going on that really exacerbate

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:17.620
the challenges that leaders face.
&gt;&gt; The fundamental

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.310
responsibility of the general manager
is to create a strategy, a way his or

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:26.835
her organization is going to achieve
success competing in the world.

00:00:26.835 --> 00:00:29.735
And then to build an organization
through which that

00:00:29.735 --> 00:00:32.791
strategy can be realized.
&gt;&gt; What they need to do is have a set of

00:00:32.791 --> 00:00:36.899
skills for diagnosing the challenges that
they're tackling and also then being able

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:41.477
to translate that into an action plan.
&gt;&gt; What I'd like to do is figure out

00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:45.197
how my business unit can contribute
to my company's strategy and

00:00:45.197 --> 00:00:49.347
align with my company's long term goals.
&gt;&gt; The main focus of this program

00:00:49.347 --> 00:00:51.927
is on the issue of what we call alignment.

00:00:51.927 --> 00:00:55.467
How do I align my strategy to
my competitive environment?

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:58.197
And how do I align my
organizational design

00:00:58.197 --> 00:01:02.540
to the strategy that I'm trying to pursue.
&gt;&gt; What is the right leadership and

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.412
what works and what doesn't work.
&gt;&gt; Great organizations are well-aligned

00:01:06.412 --> 00:01:08.050
organizations.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.140
We really bring together faculty
who have devoted their careers

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.680
to understanding the kinds of
challenges that lead our space.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.250
&gt;&gt; These are the guys that wrote the cases

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:21.650
and we're in the room with the people
who worked in the companies, so

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.718
it's fantastic
&gt;&gt; The innovation in

00:01:24.718 --> 00:01:27.300
the world right now is centered
out on Silicon Valley, and

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.010
I see Stanford as a hub for that.
&gt;&gt; I think the most exciting part that

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:34.000
many participants benefit from is designed
thinking, that really interactive and

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.880
engaged kind of process.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.580
We also have a management simulator
that participants engage in.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.610
Where they really get to understand
the challenges of teamwork and

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.660
leadership in a hands-on way that
opens eyes for a lot of people.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.070
Having the opportunity to exchange ideas,
pitch those ideas to other people

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.950
will bring new value to our participants.
&gt;&gt; A successful

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.320
leadership is not about
a cookie cutter solution.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.250
We really wanted to take you away
from just feeding you at standard

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.750
here's what all the best companies do to
saying let's give you the ability to build

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.730
your own solution set.
&gt;&gt; Managers

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:14.470
get motivated to create new ideas.
&gt;&gt; Each person came

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.030
with different perspectives.
&gt;&gt; The information of this program will

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.024
help me become a better leader.
&gt;&gt; Was really well-rounded,

00:02:20.024 --> 00:02:22.957
global experience I will
treasure rest of my life.

00:02:22.957 --> 00:02:29.209
[MUSIC]

